        "id": 1081399
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-10-12T23:43:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This might just be because I\u0027m new to HIDL, but I don\u0027t understand the asymmetry between start() and isStarted(). Why does start() return a result via a synchronous callback, while isStarted() returns its result in a more conventional way?\n\n(I\u0027m not necessarily opposed to what you have here, but I want to understand the rationale. Note that having a clear rationale will increase the odds of maintaining a uniform approach, if/when we add new methods in the future.)",
      "revId": "168a2ff9959aeb30e0aeb3945a99addd96cad8c1",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5ca192a9_562324af",
        "filename": "wifi/1.0/default/wifi.h",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 46,
      "author": {
        "id": 1074459
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-10-13T00:07:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This part of HIDL semantics is pretty annoying for a lot of people :). See b/31951482 for more.\n\nThe simple answer is that\na) any HIDL method returning a primitive type (int, bool, string, etc) will directly return a value in the generated code.\nb) Any HIDL method returning a complex/user defined type (vector, structs, etc) will be returned via callbacks in the generated code.",
      "parentUuid": "5c64320a_72b3f89f",
      "revId": "168a2ff9959aeb30e0aeb3945a99addd96cad8c1",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
